      "target": "roles",
      "cardinality": "N:N",
      "via": "user_roles",
      "description": "Users are assigned roles through the user_roles join table."
    },
    {
      "source": "roles",
      "target": "permissions",
      "cardinality": "N:N",
      "via": "role_permissions",
      "description": "Roles are composed of granular permissions through the role_permissions join table."
    }
  ],
  "external_references": [],
  "integrity_rules": [
    "Email uniqueness is strictly enforced per tenant among non-deleted user records via a partial unique index on (tenant_id, email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL (FR01, NFR05).",
    "User status transitions are constrained to ACTIVE, INACTIVE, and LOCKED via column-level CHECK constraint (FR05).",
    "Failed login attempts must be non-negative (CHECK failed_login_attempts >= 0) and reset upon successful authentication (FR03).",
    "System roles marked with is_system = true cannot be removed, and role names must be unique within a tenant via UNIQUE (tenant_id, name) (FR11).",
    "Associative records in user_roles and role_permissions enforce composite uniqueness per tenant, preventing duplicate role or permission grants (FR01, FR07, FR11).",
    "Physical referential integrity is strictly maintained within module boundaries with foreign keys referencing users(id), roles(id), and permissions(id) (NFR04)."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "System-wide permission codes are seeded into the permissions table per tenant (or associated with a designated system tenant UUID) to satisfy the strict architecture constraint requiring tenant_id on every table.",
    "Ephemeral password reset tokens, session refresh tokens, and authentication blacklists are managed in Redis with automatic TTL eviction in accordance with NFR06, requiring no persistent database tables.",
    "Email addresses are normalized to lowercase prior to persistence to ensure case-insensitive uniqueness per tenant."
  ],
  "data_model_summary": "The Users module data model provides tenant-isolated user identity management, credential authentication, and Role-Based Access Control (RBAC). It consists of five tables: users (holding credentials, profile data, account lifecycle status, and lockout metadata), roles (holding tenant-defined and system roles), permissions (cataloging code-level capability codes), user_roles (associative table mapping users to roles), and role_permissions (associative table granting permissions to roles). All tables include UUIDv7 primary keys, tenant discriminators, and UTC timestamps."
}